!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/38/io/ltr8/http/meta-service.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  A meta layer for describing a service: what it can be asked to do (methods, grouped into interfaces)
  and how HTTP exposes that (an api of endpoints). A schema governed by this one is a service description,
  and `TsonApiDescription` reads it.

  One vocabulary, two constructors -- and only two. `interface` and `api` are the entries a governed schema
  writes, so they are namespace types (§4.1): an entry applying one is a map from a name to a value, and a
  value, never a type. An interface maps method names to methods; an api maps path templates to resources.
  Everything inside them -- `method`, `resource`, `endpoint`, `operation`, `binding`, `document`, `exemption`
  -- is an ordinary RECORD, because it only ever appears as a map value. A record composes (`operation =>
  endpoint & signature`), is admitted as a subtype where its base is expected (§7.2), and is never a
  constructor anyone applies at entry level.

    orders => !interface {
      @safe get_order => { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
      place_order     => { request: new_order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found] }
    }

    @implements:[orders]
    orders_api => !api {
      "/orders"      => { POST => !binding { method: orders.place_order  status: 201 } }
      "/orders/{id}" => { GET  => !binding { method: orders.get_order } }
    }

  Methods are keys inside their interface, and endpoints are values two maps down -- keyed by path, then
  by verb -- so neither is a global entry: two interfaces may both declare `get_order`, and an api needs no
  invented operation names. A method's qualified name is its interface's entry, then its key.

  What an api claims about the interfaces behind it is ANNOTATION on its entry, written before the name:
  `@implements` and `@not_bound`. A value of a namespace type is the map alone, with no fields beside its
  keys, so a fact about the api as a whole has nowhere else to be written. An interface lists its methods and
  nothing more; there is no inheritance between interfaces, and an api implementing two names both.

  Three ways to use it:
    interface only    an `interface`; nothing HTTP in the document.
    web service only  an `api` of `!operation`s, each carrying its signature INLINE.
    both              an `interface`, and an `api` that `@implements` it with `!binding`s naming its methods.
  An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value -- a schema document served as bytes, a page -- is a
  `!document`, stating its media type where the others state a response type.

  THE POSITIONAL FORM, AND WHAT IT COSTS. `resource` has exactly one bare REQUIRED field, so §5.6 lets
  the map stand in for it: `"/orders" => { GET => ... }` is `"/orders" => { endpoints: { GET => ... } }`,
  and the tag is optional under the path key. It reads far better and the examples use it. The cost is a
  versioning one and it is the reason this is written down: adding a SECOND required field to `resource`
  withdraws the positional form from it, and every document written in it stops parsing. So a new field
  there is optional or it is a new type.

  WHAT THE RESOLVER CHECKS, AND WHAT THE READER DOES. A relation that names something is a reference.
  A binding's `method` is a `ref<method>` the resolver judges at schema load: a qualified name walking an
  entry and its key -- `orders.place_order` -- so a name nothing declares, one naming the wrong kind of thing,
  or a method key the interface does not hold is refused there, and the qualified name says which interface
  it means. The resolver does not judge a reference inside an annotation's value, so `@implements` and an
  exemption's `method` are typed as references here, saying what they mean, and `TsonApiDescription` checks
  them itself: each `@implements` name is an entry applying `interface`, and each exemption names a method an
  implemented interface holds. What relates two things beyond naming one is the reader's too, when it reads a
  description, and an unsound description has no model: a binding's method belonging to an interface the api
  implements, the `@implements` claim, the placement of a request record over a path, a verb or a status
  against what the method declares, an error type fixing its status, and no two endpoints sharing a verb and
  a path.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    The transport-neutral contract of one callable thing. A plain record on purpose: it is what `method`
    and an inline `operation` share.

    `request` absent means the call takes nothing; `response` absent means it returns nothing (an HTTP
    204). `errors` lists BUSINESS errors -- the request was schema-valid and the domain said no -- each
    a type composing `problem`. A transport's own refusals (a body failing its schema, an Accept it
    cannot satisfy) are not listed: every call has them and the error boundary answers them.
    """
  signature => {
    request?:         type_kind
    response?:        type_kind
    errors?:          [type_kind]
  }

  @doc:"""
    A method: a signature and nothing more. Its NAME is the key it sits under in an interface. A record,
    not a constructor: it is only ever a map value. The empty trailing body is what makes it a type of its
    own -- `method => signature` would be an alias, and an alias is a hop to its target, so the values
    would read back as signatures.
    """
  method => signature & {}

  @doc:"""
    An interface: methods keyed by name, and nothing else. A namespace type, so an entry applying it is the
    map of methods itself, and a qualified name enters it -- a `ref<method>` reaches a method as
    `orders.place_order`, the entry and then the key. A method is a map VALUE under its name, so its name is
    scoped to the interface, and annotations written before the key -- `@doc`, `@summary`, `@safe`,
    `@idempotent` -- document it and state its facts.
    """
  interface => namespace & { key_type: = method_name  value_type: = method }

  @doc:"Verb, not method: `method` is the constructor above, and `{ method: POST }` helps nobody."
  http_verb => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    The naming roles of the namespaces this layer borrows -- one per namespace, as the kernel declares
    `type_name`, `field_name` and `param_name` over `identifier`. A key typed by a role enforces that
    namespace's grammar and names the namespace in the refusal ("'method_name': 'place order': U+0020 cannot
    appear in an identifier"), where `type_name` would misname the namespace doing the refusing. A namespace
    type's key is a name, so its role is an identifier family: where the authority's grammar is wider than
    TSON's identifier, the role is an `identifier_type` widened with `start_add` and `continue_add`, as
    `path_template` is. A map field's key may still be a text family carrying a `pattern`, as `header_name`
    is; a `pattern` is I-Regexp, matched against the whole token, so no anchors.

    A method's name: an identifier in its interface's namespace, not a type name.
    """
  method_name => identifier

  @doc:"""
    An api's key: an absolute path written as a URI template (RFC 6570) at levels 1 and 2, whose variables
    name fields of the endpoint's request record. `{id}` expands a value percent-encoded; `{+schemaPath}`,
    reserved expansion, leaves its `/` unencoded, which is what an identity path with slashes in it needs.
    The fragment operator is not admitted, since a server never receives a fragment, and nor are levels 3 and
    4 -- several variables to an expression, query forms, prefixes and explode -- which describe expansion
    rather than a route a server can match. A variable name is ASCII letters, digits and `_`.

    An identifier family, because it keys a namespace: it starts with `/`, and continues with identifier
    characters and RFC 3986's other path characters -- unreserved, sub-delimiters but `'`, `:`, `@`, a
    percent-encoded octet -- plus a template's braces and `+`. The `pattern` holds the structure the
    character set cannot: braces paired around one variable, and `%` followed by two hex digits. Being a
    name, a path key meets [TSON-DATA] §8.2's name hygiene too, so a mixed-script path is refused.
    """
  path_template => !identifier_type {
    start:        NONE
    start_add:    "/"
    continue_add: "/{}+%-._~!$&()*,;=:@"
    pattern:      "/([^%{}]|%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}|\\{[+]?[A-Za-z0-9_]+\\})*"
  }

  @doc:"""
    A header field name: an RFC 9110 token, compared without case as RFC 9110 §5.1 has it -- held folded, so
    `Idempotency-Key` is `idempotency-key`, the form HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 put on the wire. It keys a map field
    rather than a namespace, so it is a text family with a `pattern`.
    """
  header_name => !text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+"  normalization: NFKC_CASEFOLD }

  @doc:"""
    Where a request record's fields go, and the place where an interface and the web service over it
    are allowed to look different. The verb and the path are not here: they are the keys an operation
    sits under, in its resource and its api.

    A method has one request record, and the endpoint distributes that record's fields across the HTTP
    request: a `{segment}` in the resource's path binds the field of that name; `query` names fields
    carried as query parameters; `headers` maps a field to the header that carries it; `body` names ONE
    field carried as the whole body, unwrapped. Whatever is left is the remainder, and the remainder is
    the body -- unless the verb is GET or HEAD, or `body` already named a field, in which case it is the
    query. Every field lands in exactly one place, and a field carried in the path, the query or a
    header must be a scalar -- a URL segment cannot carry a record. That is what makes the mapping
    checkable: a field placed twice, a name the request does not declare, or a container in the path is
    a bad endpoint rather than a surprise.

    There is no parameter list. A parameter-only endpoint declares a one-field request record
    (`schema_ref => { schemaPath: text }`) and binds it from the path -- a method that takes anything
    takes exactly ONE record, which is what lets one rule serve every endpoint and the check see every
    field. `signature.request` is optional because a method may take nothing at all, and such an endpoint
    admits no placement: a `{segment}` on it is refused, having no field to bind. A request that is
    present and is not a record is refused too, since there would be nothing to distribute.
    """
  placement => {
    query?:   [field_name]
    headers?: {field_name => header_name}
    body?:    field_name
  }

  @doc:"""
    What sits under a verb, under a path, in an api: the base record of `operation`, `binding` and `document`,
    carrying what each has -- the placement of a request record, the status of the response, and the
    encodings the endpoint speaks. A value at an `endpoint`-typed slot is tagged with the subtype it is and
    admitted as that subtype (§7.2); a reader binds the base to a type with no bare form, so an untagged value
    is refused naming the subtypes. The tag is the whole difference between the one-declaration form and the
    two-declaration form, and it is read before anything else.

    `status` is the status of the success response; there is no list of responses, because a method has one
    response type. An error's status is the `status` member its own type pins --
    `sku_not_found => problem & { status: = 404  sku: text }` -- so an error type an endpoint names MUST fix
    it, and `TsonApiDescription` refuses one that does not. A transport's own refusals -- a body failing its
    schema, an Accept nothing satisfies -- are not listed: every endpoint has them, and the error boundary
    answers each with a `problem`.

    Documentation is annotation, not fields: `@doc` before the verb key is the long form and `@summary` the
    short one, both surviving into the read value as the key's annotations, since an endpoint is a map value
    and a map key carries annotations. A binding's method carries its own on its key in the interface.

    Deliberately absent, each a decision: security schemes, response headers, links, callbacks,
    examples, tags, servers, multi-media-type negotiation, and more than one success response. Adding
    one is a later version under §3.5 once this is published, never an edit -- and a new kind of endpoint
    is a new record composing this one, admitted wherever an `endpoint` is.
    """
  endpoint => placement & {
    status?:     status_code ~ 200
    encodings?:  [encoding]
  }

  @doc:"""
    An encoding an endpoint's payloads travel in: TSON text, or TSON's JSON encoding ([TSON-JSON]). Every
    endpoint speaks TSON, so `encodings` says what it speaks beside it, absent meaning TSON alone. An encoding
    and not a media type, because that is the contract: application/tson+json and application/json are two
    labels for the one JSON encoding, and which label a response carries is Accept's to negotiate rather than
    the description's to fix.
    """
  encoding => !enum [TSON JSON]

  @doc:"A media type (RFC 9110 §8.3.1): a type and a subtype, each a token, with no parameters."
  media_type => !text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+/[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+" }

  @doc:"""
    An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value: a schema document served as the bytes it was published as, a
    page. It states the `media_type` of what it answers where an operation states a response type, and may
    still take a request record to place and name business errors. Its success body is not judged as a
    value, only labelled -- the media type is the contract.
    """
  document => endpoint & {
    request?:   type_kind
    errors?:    [type_kind]
    media_type: media_type
  }

  @doc:"""
    The short form of an endpoint's or a method's documentation, written before its key --
    `@summary:"Place an order." POST => !binding { ... }`. Declared as the kernel declares `doc`;
    `@doc` remains the long form. Not a field, because documentation is metadata about a declaration rather
    than part of what it declares, and an annotation type declared here reaches every schema this layer
    governs (§6, one hop).
    """
  summary => @annotation text

  @doc:"""
    Two facts about a method that a caller composing calls needs -- an agent planning a batch, deciding
    what may be retried or reordered -- and has no HTTP verb to read them from. Bare markers before the
    method's key: `@safe get_order => { ... }`, `@idempotent cancel_order => { ... }`. `@safe` implies
    `@idempotent`. Annotations rather than fields because they describe the method, not its request or
    response; the HTTP projection CHECKS its verb against them (GET must be safe; PUT and DELETE
    idempotent) and never derives one from the other.
    """
  safe       => @annotation void
  idempotent => @annotation void

  @doc:"An endpoint with its signature INLINE -- the one-declaration form, for an api with no interface behind it."
  operation => endpoint & signature

  @doc:"""
    An endpoint whose signature is a METHOD's -- the two-declaration form. `method` is a reference the
    resolver judges, its qualified name saying which interface declares it (`orders.get_order`, the
    interface's entry and then the method's key), so no binding is ambiguous between two interfaces declaring
    one name. That the interface is one the api implements is the reader's check.
    """
  binding => endpoint & {
    method: ref<method>
  }

  @doc:"""
    One path: its endpoints, keyed by verb. A record with one bare required field, so the map stands in
    for it and the tag is optional under the path key -- `"/orders" => { POST => !binding { ... } }` and
    `"/orders" => !resource { endpoints: { ... } }` read alike.
    """
  resource => {
    endpoints: {http_verb => endpoint}
  }

  @doc:"""
    Why a claimed method has no endpoint here: a method documented ahead of being built, or served by another
    process behind the same proxy. `reason` is required, because an exemption somebody has to justify in a
    greppable string is a decision where a boolean is a hole. `method` names exactly the method it covers, as
    a binding's does -- `orders.refund` -- so it can never quietly cover a second interface's method of the
    same name. Typed `ref<method>` for what it means; held inside an annotation's value, it is not judged by
    the resolver, so `TsonApiDescription` refuses one naming no method of an interface the api implements.
    A list of records rather than a map keyed by method: a map key is a name in the map's own key family,
    never a reference.
    """
  exemption => {
    method: ref<method>
    reason: text
  }

  @doc:"""
    A web service: resources keyed by path template, each holding endpoints keyed by verb -- the shape
    OpenAPI's `paths` has, arrived at from the key types rather than copied. A namespace type, so an entry
    applying it is the map of paths itself. A path key is the URL namespace's own grammar -- a
    `path_template` -- which is why an endpoint needs no invented name and why `/orders/{id}` needs no
    identifier minted from it. Nothing references an endpoint, so no qualified name walks a path key.

    What the api claims about the interfaces behind it is written as annotations before its entry's name,
    `@implements:[orders] @not_bound:[{ method: orders.refund  reason: "..." }] orders_api => !api { ... }`,
    because a value of a namespace type is the map alone.
    """
  api => namespace & { key_type: = path_template  value_type: = resource }

  @doc:"""
    The interfaces an api binds, and a CLAIM the reader holds it to: every method of every interface named
    has a `binding` naming it, or an `exemption` in `@not_bound` giving the reason it does not. The claim is
    held per (interface, method), not per name: two implemented interfaces may both declare `get_order`, and
    binding one of them says nothing about the other. An api with no `@implements` is a web service on its
    own terms, its endpoints `!operation`s. Typed `ref<interface>` for what it means; held in an annotation's
    value, it is not judged by the resolver, so `TsonApiDescription` refuses a name that is no entry applying
    `interface`.
    """
  implements => @annotation [ref<interface>]

  @doc:"The methods an api's `@implements` claims and does not bind, each with the reason why."
  not_bound => @annotation [exemption]
}
